WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:08.160
Ready to go. The media and a fair number of&nbsp;
politicians act like the US and China are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:12.160
at each other's economic throats. In fact,&nbsp;
the US probably helped China more at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:15.520
end of the Trump administration than it did&nbsp;
at the beginning. It's true we've hit China&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:20.640
with tariffs. The tariffs are overblown. In&nbsp;
2016, Donald Trump attacked the trade deficit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:25.280
for sending billions of dollars to China. The&nbsp;
US-China deficit was 310 billion dollars then;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:30.880
in 2020 it was 288 billion dollars. Much more&nbsp;
money is headed to China in a different way.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:36.720
American holdings of Chinese stocks and bonds&nbsp;
were worth 104 billion dollars at the end of 2016.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:42.240
At the end of 2020 they were worth 287 billion.&nbsp;
Including investment running through the Cayman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:47.920
Islands adds 300 billion more in just those&nbsp;
four years. The trade deficit drop means nothing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:53.120
and the US government isn't even sure how this&nbsp;
money is ultimately being used: to repress human&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:57.920
rights or help the Chinese military are ugly&nbsp;
possibilities. Some is certainly used to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:03.200
china more competitive. There are many plans to&nbsp;
outcompete China. None of them will work if huge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:08.160
amounts of American money keep helping China&nbsp;
outcompete us. Should the US be doing more to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:12.800
confront China? Let us know in our poll. Also let&nbsp;
us know what other topics you'd like our scholars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:17.360
to cover in 60 seconds, and be sure to like and&nbsp;
subscribe for more research and videos from AEI.

